1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:07,220
You think you've explored medicine. In reality, you're standing in the parking lot of Disney World bragging about how fun

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:12,990
the pavement is. You've taken pictures of the asphalt. You've written dissertations about the parking stripes.

3
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:22,160
You've never gone inside. After a century of pharmacology, most beneficial effects of molecules on diseases remain unexplored.

4
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:30,360
Not because biology is constrained. Because clinical trials are slow, expensive, and run by people who think "urgency" is

5
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:37,800
a type of perfume. Here's the math. It's painful but brief, like a flu shot administered by someone who hates you.

6
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:48,620
Your Tiny Sandbox. Here is everything humanity considers "medicine" after 5,000 years of civilization: Twenty thousand prescription

7
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:56,080
products are approved in the U.S. Sounds impressive until you realize most are just "Tylenol, but blue.".

8
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:01,040
These unique ingredient products contain only one thousand six hundred fifty compounds.

9
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:09,340
Fewer ingredients than a decent French bakery uses. Seven thousand five hundred investigational drug compounds have passed

10
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:16,000
Phase One globally. Progress! The F.D.A. Generally Recognized As Safe, or G.R.A.S.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,850
list contains six hundred thirty-five substances. These are things you could eat right now without dying.

12
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:31,960
Low bar. Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

13
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:36,020
That's it. That's your entire medicine cabinet after five millennia.

14
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:43,760
The Target List (Ways Your Body Breaks). The International Classification of Diseases, or I.C.D.

15
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:48,820
Ten, lists fourteen thousand codes for ways your meat can malfunction.

16
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:53,440
Humanity's most comprehensive to-do list, and you're ignoring it.

17
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:59,760
After consolidation, there are one thousand trial-relevant diseases worth actually fixing.

18
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:06,240
The Math You're Ignoring. Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds.

19
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:14,540
Multiply by one thousand diseases. This gives you the "Combinatorial Space," which is fancy talk for "stuff you could test

20
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:21,260
tomorrow if you weren't busy filling out forms.". Nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals

21
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:25,520
nine point five million plausible drug-condition combinations.

22
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:33,380
What You've Actually Tested. There are one thousand seven hundred fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

23
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:40,840
Your entire medical canon. Thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

24
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:47,700
The rest never get a second date. Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

25
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:53,880
(You did. You learned nothing from them.). Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

26
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:01,800
The Scorecard. Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested divided by nine point five million possibilities equals

27
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:12,240
zero point three four two percent. where: Humanity has explored less than one percent of the theoretically testable drug-disease

28
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:16,540
space. You are the detective who searched one drawer and declared the case cold.

29
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:22,320
It's like reading the first page of War and Peace and writing a book report called "It's About a Party."

30
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:28,130
Technically accurate. Cosmically insufficient. Is The Untested Stuff Useful?

31
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:34,420
You might argue, "Maybe the other 99% is useless!". Biology would like a word.

32
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:43,640
Undrugged Targets: Mapping three hundred and fifty thousand plus clinical trials showed that only 12% of the human interactome

33
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:49,180
has ever been targeted by drugs. You're ignoring 88% of your own biology.

34
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:53,660
It's like having a 100-room mansion and living exclusively in the bathroom.

35
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:59,440
Polypharmacology: Drugs are messy. They hit multiple targets.

36
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:03,340
F.D.A.-approved drugs often bind to things you didn't intend.

37
00:04:03,820 --> 00:04:08,540
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that nobody bothered to notice.

38
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:13,020
Every headache pill might cure Alzheimer's. You won't know until you check.

39
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:19,560
You're not checking. Network Overlap: Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

40
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:27,280
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share biological pathways. The cure for your grandmother's dementia might be sitting in your grandfather's

41
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:34,000
medicine cabinet. But you'll never find out, because testing it would require paperwork that takes longer than Grandma has

42
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:42,000
left. The Universe is Big. You Are Small. So far we only counted molecules humanity already has.

43
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:47,240
The stuff in your cupboards. If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets embarrassing.

44
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:54,340
Chemical Space: Estimated ten to the twenty-third power to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules.

45
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:59,530
That's more candidates than there are atoms in your body. Or stars in the galaxy.

46
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:09,480
Or excuses from your regulatory agencies. Virtual Libraries: ZINC twenty-two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual

47
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:18,040
molecules. You could order them online. You haven't. Synthesized: You have made less than ten to the power of negative forty

48
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:25,980
of possible molecules. Tested divided by possible equals approximately ten to the power of negative twenty to ten to the

49
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:31,650
power of negative fifty. Effectively zero. You haven't even started.

50
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:38,940
This is like exploring Earth by looking at one grain of sand on one beach in New Jersey, then declaring you've "seen the

51
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:43,320
world." You haven't. You've seen a grain of sand. In New Jersey.

52
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:51,760
Why You Are Still Sick. It's not because science is hard. It's because you've built a system that is allergic to answers.

53
00:05:52,800 --> 00:06:02,020
Trials Cost Too Much: A typical Phase Two to Three randomized controlled trial, or R.C.T., costs thirty to one hundred million

54
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:05,960
dollars (Median cost per patient forty-one thousand dollars).

55
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:11,060
At that price, you only test "sure things." Safe bets. Boring bets.

56
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:19,000
Bets that don't cure cancer. Clinical trials are a velvet-rope nightclub where the bouncer is a regulatory agency and the

57
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:28,220
cover charge is a hundred million dollars. Herd Mentality: Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due

58
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:34,940
to preferential attachment dynamics. Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

59
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:40,790
It's like if every explorer only went to France because France already had good reviews on TripAdvisor.

60
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:46,600
Meanwhile, undiscovered continents full of cures sit there, undiscovered.

61
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:56,940
No Profit. Most molecules have no patent. No patent equals no one hundred million dollar trial equals no approval.

62
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:06,440
The cure for cancer might be aspirin plus vitamin D. Nobody will ever test it because you can't patent aspirin plus vitamin

63
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:11,580
D. The molecule that saves your life has to make money first.

64
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:19,380
If it can't, it won't. You die. Quarterly earnings go up. Neglect.

65
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,560
If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

66
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:32,680
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against quarterly

67
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:39,120
earnings and found you wanting. Sorry about your death. It was a business decision.

68
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:49,520
How Long to Explore Everything? At the current pace of three thousand three hundred trials per year, how long would it take

69
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:57,500
to systematically test therapeutic possibilities? Tier 1: Single Compounds (Most Conservative).

70
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:07,380
Testing every known safe compound against every major disease: Nine point five million combinations divided by three thousand

71
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:13,300
three hundred trials per year equals two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

72
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:19,100
That's almost three thousand years to explore compounds we already know are safe.

73
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,320
By that math, we won't finish until the year five thousand.

74
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:33,820
Tier 2: Combination Therapies (Still Defensible). Modern medicine increasingly relies on multi-drug cocktails.

75
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:41,419
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely use drug combinations.

76
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:47,620
So the real question isn't "does this one drug work?" It's "which combinations work?"

77
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:57,300
Testing pairwise combinations of safe compounds: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

78
00:08:57,300 --> 00:09:02,400
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

79
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:10,620
That's longer than Homo sapiens has existed as a species. And that's using only compounds with established safety data,

80
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:16,610
testing only pairs. Not triplets. God help you if you want to try triplets.

81
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:27,520
Tier 3: The Full Chemical Universe (Illustrative). Beyond known compounds lies the chemical space of ten to the twenty-third

82
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:35,800
to ten to the sixtieth drug-like molecules. At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction would take

83
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:42,120
longer than the universe has existed. The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

84
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:48,300
You need more time than that. To test pills. Pills that already exist.

85
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:54,400
We haven't even started. "But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?".

86
00:09:55,740 --> 00:10:01,540
Some will argue "diminishing returns": the more we search, the harder each new discovery becomes.

87
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:07,730
This assumes we've already picked the low-hanging fruit. We haven't picked ANY fruit.

88
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:11,310
We're still in the parking lot arguing about whether fruit exists.

89
00:10:12,340 --> 00:10:17,820
Diminishing returns only kicks in after you've explored a significant fraction of the space.

90
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:27,080
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new about human biology, improving your ability to

91
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:32,780
predict what works next. Learning compounds. Depletion is negligible.

92
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:38,300
You are nowhere near the bottom of the barrel because you have not yet located the barrel.

93
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:48,120
The math: even with weak learning, diminishing returns only matters after exploring approximately thirty-seven percent of

94
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:57,020
therapeutic space. For combination therapies, that crossover is approximately seven million years away at current pace.

95
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:01,600
You cannot have diminishing returns when you've barely started looking.

96
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:12,520
See the formal analysis for the equations. The Fix. The Oxford RECOVERY Trial (COVID nineteen) proved that large-scale pragmatic

97
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:17,760
trials can run for about five hundred dollars and deliver results in less than one hundred days.

98
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:23,940
Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars. Read those numbers again.

99
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:32,420
One of them is the reason people are dying. With a decentralized framework for drug assessment, or D.F.D.A.

100
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:42,180
(the decentralized FDA), trial capacity increases by twelve point three times: Nine point five zero million combinations

101
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:48,060
divided by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year equals two hundred thirty-four years.

102
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:53,300
Still a long time, but at least it's a number with fewer digits than a phone book.

103
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:59,980
And the first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries (the low-hanging fruit that current trial scarcity

104
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:03,580
forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room).

105
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:12,940
More importantly, D.F.D.A. (the decentralized FDA) builds permanent infrastructure for systematic therapeutic exploration.

106
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:20,820
The goal isn't to "finish." It's to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether to form

107
00:12:20,820 --> 00:12:25,660
committees to review the proposal to consider them. The Bottom Line.

108
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:34,160
Humanity has empirically tested less than one percent of the drug-disease relationships that can be tested using existing

109
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:44,480
safe compounds. If you include the larger chemical universe, humanity has explored roughly one-quadrillionth to one-quintillionth

110
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:50,280
of what is possible (ten to the power of negative twelve to ten to the power of negative fifty).

111
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:56,780
There are numbers so small that mathematicians get depressed looking at them.

112
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:02,940
These are those numbers. The overwhelming majority of therapeutic potential remains untouched.

113
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:08,780
Not because cures are impossible. Because trials are too expensive and too slow.

114
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:17,040
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine

115
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:25,200
point nine nine nine percent of medical space. You're not in the parking lot because there's nothing inside Disney World.

116
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:31,760
You're in the parking lot because the entrance fee is one hundred million dollars and requires seventeen years of paperwork.

117
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:36,380
People are dying in the parking lot. The rides are right there.

118
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:40,380
You can hear the screaming. (Fun screaming. Cure screaming.).

119
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:44,380
The cures are inside. Open the gate.
